WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.561
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: Claude solves two open problems in the physics of black holes.

2
00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:13.378
Claude solves two open problems in the physics of black holes.

3
00:00:13.828 --> 00:00:23.701
Claude Fable 5.2 has solved two open problems in theoretical physics. Both concern black holes and the search for a theory of quantum gravity.

4
00:00:24.151 --> 00:00:28.879
From Anthropic Research, published November 17, 2026.

5
00:00:29.879 --> 00:00:46.301
Quote: Claude has solved two open problems in theoretical physics. Both concern black holes and the search for a theory of quantum gravity. In the first, it completed a calculation that physicists set up more than 20 years ago, but which had never been solved.

6
00:00:46.421 --> 00:01:09.376
Claude’s result showed that a sudden transition associated with the formation of black holes persists even where spacetime no longer has a definite shape. In the second, Claude found the exact limit of the simplest version of a technique used to test candidate theories of quantum gravity. This post explains both findings, first in plain terms and then in more technical detail.

7
00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:12.269
The preprints are available here and here. End quote.

8
00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:13.913
Introduction.

9
00:01:14.363 --> 00:01:34.287
Modern physics rests on two frameworks: general relativity and quantum mechanics. General relativity describes very large things, like stars; quantum mechanics describes very small things, such as atoms. Both are astonishingly good at predicting how the universe behaves, but they’re mathematically incompatible.

10
00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:50.765
Finding a theory that unites them, “quantum gravity”, is one of the field’s biggest goals. Black holes are a natural testing ground, because they’re one of the few areas where both relativity and quantum mechanics matter, they pack immense mass into a very small space.

11
00:01:51.215 --> 00:02:11.619
One of the most useful tools for studying quantum gravity is called holographic duality. The real universe has three dimensions of space and one of time, which relativity combines into four-dimensional spacetime. But physicists studying quantum gravity often work with simpler model universes that they can understand in more detail.

12
00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:27.942
The basic idea of holographic duality is that some of these theoretical universes can be described in two ways: as a complete universe with gravity and spacetime, and in quantum terms, with no gravity at all, “written” on the universe’s outer boundary.

13
00:02:28.062 --> 00:02:44.100
Just as a real hologram encodes a 3D image on a 2D plane, the quantum model encodes all the information of the universe in one dimension fewer (a universe with four dimensions of space, for instance, can be fully described by a quantum theory with three).

14
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:51.589
This lets physicists turn hard questions about gravity into questions about quantum physics, which are sometimes easier to answer.

15
00:02:52.039 --> 00:03:15.755
The first problem Claude solved concerns what happens when you heat up one of these theoretical universes. At low temperatures, the universe fills up with a gas of particles. Above a certain temperature, a black hole forms instead. In 1983, Stephen Hawking and Don Page showed that this switch is abrupt: it’s a phase transition, similar to how water suddenly boils at 100°C.

16
00:03:15.875 --> 00:03:38.372
But Hawking and Page’s calculation relies on general relativity, which holds only when the universe is vast, compared with the tiny scale at which that description breaks down. As the theoretical universe shrinks toward that smaller scale, spacetime loses any definite shape and becomes blurry, and nobody knows how to do the calculation on the gravity side of the duality.

17
00:03:38.492 --> 00:03:43.496
On the quantum side, though, the same situation can be calculated, at least in principle.

18
00:03:43.946 --> 00:04:03.072
In 1999, physicists found that there was a phase transition in that regime as well. But they didn’t know whether it was abrupt, like Hawking and Page’s, or smooth. In 2003, a group of physicists set out the calculation that would settle the question, but it was extremely complex and was never completed.

19
00:04:03.192 --> 00:04:16.800
Claude did it by running a small research group of copies of itself, in which it worked out almost every piece of the calculation independently and checked its math against known results. It showed that the phase transition is abrupt.

20
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:28.133
The same sudden switch appears at both extremes, suggesting that black hole formation isn’t just a feature of relativity but is actually built into the underlying quantum theory.

21
00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:44.029
The second problem concerns a simplified theoretical universe with two dimensions of space instead of three. This universe is straightforward enough to study in detail, but still has black holes and other mysteries similar to those of our own universe.

22
00:04:44.149 --> 00:05:02.342
Even in this simpler universe, physicists don’t know how to create a full theory of quantum gravity, so they approach it from the other direction: rather than building a single, correct theory, they try to rule out wrong ones by showing that they are internally inconsistent. This method is called bootstrapping.

23
00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:17.251
Since 2009, physicists have used a version of this method to set a ceiling on how heavy the lightest black hole in such a universe could be. Any theory that breaks the ceiling is ruled out, and each lowering of the ceiling narrows the search.

24
00:05:17.371 --> 00:05:28.696
Results from numerical calculations hinted at where the best possible ceiling lay, but they couldn’t prove it, and the best proven ceiling was well short of what the numerical results suggested.

25
00:05:29.146 --> 00:05:49.876
Claude Fable 5.2 noticed a pattern in the numerical results that physicists had missed and borrowed a method from elsewhere in math and physics to calculate the precise ceiling for the simplest version of this technique and prove that it could not be lowered. It doesn’t identify the correct theory, but it may point physicists in the right direction.

26
00:05:50.326 --> 00:06:03.431
Neither result describes our own universe directly, and neither has practical applications, but both contribute to a long-running theoretical effort to understand how quantum mechanics and relativity fit together.

27
00:06:03.881 --> 00:06:10.215
In the rest of this post, we explain in more detail what each problem was and what Claude did to solve them.

28
00:06:11.015 --> 00:06:12.609
A note about Claude’s methods.

29
00:06:13.059 --> 00:06:28.987
In August, an Anthropic researcher, Hongbin Chen, was testing Claude’s capabilities as a side project. He asked Claude to draw up a list of interesting open problems in theoretical physics, specifically focusing on black holes and the bootstrap method.

30
00:06:29.107 --> 00:06:45.447
Claude came back hours later with 22 candidates, and Hongbin set up separate agents to work on each one; they could read each other’s progress posts and collaborate when working on related problems. The agents ran in the background, with no special setup, for about six weeks.

31
00:06:45.897 --> 00:07:08.173
The agents solved the two problems in this post. One was the Hawking-Page phase transition, the question of when a hot gas turns into a black hole. Weeks after one of the agents reached a solution, a physicist, who had independently used Claude to tackle the same problem, shared his result, and a colleague realized that Hongbin’s agent had reached the same answer earlier.

32
00:07:08.293 --> 00:07:12.559
The two solutions used different methods, so they were a useful check on each other.

33
00:07:13.009 --> 00:07:33.288
The modular bootstrap result came from several agents. The one that was assigned the problem spotted the pattern in the numerical results when surveying the bootstrap literature, and another one, which was working on a mathematically related problem about how tightly spheres can be packed into a high-dimensional space, turned that pattern into a method.

34
00:07:33.408 --> 00:07:53.271
It found that this method reproduced a sphere-packing result recently proved by an OpenAI model. Claude then applied the same method to the bootstrap problem, which an earlier Claude agent had tried, and failed, to solve in 61 parallel attempts. Two other agents also independently arrived at the same answer.

35
00:07:53.391 --> 00:08:11.471
One half of the final proof, showing that the ceiling can’t be lowered, follows the strategy of the OpenAI proof. In September, the results were formally verified in Lean, a computerized proof checker. Claude then wrote the paper with minimal guidance, and a physicist checked every main result.

36
00:08:12.271 --> 00:08:13.776
The Hawking-Page transition.

37
00:08:14.226 --> 00:08:20.490
To understand the first problem, we need to start with something much more familiar: a walk through New York City.

38
00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:42.400
Figure 1: Two equally good routes from Penn Station (A) to the High Line entrance (B). In theory, a quantum particle can take both simultaneously. Image description: Street map of Midtown Manhattan showing two routes from Penn Station (A) to the High Line (B). Path 1, in red, goes west and then south; Path 2, in blue, goes south and then west.

39
00:08:42.850 --> 00:09:00.717
Imagine that you’re heading from Penn Station to the High Line entrance on West 30th Street and 10th Avenue, and there are two equally good routes. You could walk west along West 33rd Street and turn south, or head south first and then walk west along West 30th Street. Either way, you have to pick one.

40
00:09:00.837 --> 00:09:06.335
In the quantum world, though, that’s not true: a particle can take both routes simultaneously.

41
00:09:06.785 --> 00:09:24.924
This is most clearly demonstrated in the famous double-slit experiment. In this test, you fire particles, one at a time, at a barrier cut with two narrow slits, and record where, on a screen behind it, each one lands. Each particle lands at a particular point on the screen, as a particle should.

42
00:09:25.044 --> 00:09:33.062
But as you fire more of them, they build up a pattern of stripes that would only be possible if each one had passed through both slits, like a wave.

43
00:09:33.512 --> 00:09:53.106
Figure 2: The double-slit experiment. When electrons are fired one at a time from a source (A) toward a barrier with two slits, each lands at a single point on the screen. The hits build up a pattern of stripes that is only possible if each one passed through both slits. Image description: Diagram of the double-slit experiment.

44
00:09:53.226 --> 00:10:05.938
Two paths lead from an electron source (A) through two slits in a barrier to the same point (B) on a screen. Beside the screen, individual electron hits build up into a pattern of bright and dark stripes.

45
00:10:06.388 --> 00:10:28.212
To predict what a quantum particle will do more generally, physicists have to add up every path it could possibly take, known as its possible histories. Not every history counts equally; some contribute far more to the sum than others. It’s an extravagant and mysterious way to describe how a single particle behaves, but it’s also one of the most successful ideas in physics.

46
00:10:28.662 --> 00:10:46.181
This approach, known as the “sum over histories,” is well established for particles and anything made of them. But what about gravity? Einstein showed that gravity is a consequence of curved spacetime: objects with mass bend space and time around them, and we feel that bending as gravity.

47
00:10:46.301 --> 00:11:04.376
In a quantum theory of gravity, therefore, what gets summed over is the shape of spacetime itself, rather than just the paths of particles moving through it. To make predictions in a quantum theory of gravity, we would have to add up every shape that the universe could possibly take, and nobody yet knows how to do that.

48
00:11:04.496 --> 00:11:08.396
Much of modern quantum gravity research is an attempt to work it out.

49
00:11:08.846 --> 00:11:29.131
Hawking and Page found a case where this sum is simple enough to handle but still interesting. To explain Hawking and Page’s work, we need to imagine a different kind of universe, called anti-de Sitter spacetime. Imagine you’re standing in a giant bowl. No matter how hard you throw an object, it will always roll back to the place you threw it from.

50
00:11:29.581 --> 00:11:49.731
Our universe isn’t like that. The difference comes down to a quantity called the cosmological constant, which describes the energy of empty space. In our universe, this energy is positive, which makes space expand ever faster. In anti-de Sitter spacetime, the energy is negative, so everything is pulled back to where it started.

51
00:11:49.851 --> 00:11:59.529
Hawking and Page chose this universe for their calculation because of how it behaves when it heats up: the bowl keeps heat in so it can stay at a steady temperature.

52
00:11:59.979 --> 00:12:21.927
At any given temperature, two histories matter far more than all the others. Here, a “history” means an entire version of the universe: a particular shape of spacetime, along with everything in it. In one, the bowl is filled with an ordinary gas of particles, including gravitons, the particles believed to carry gravity. In the other, it contains a black hole.

53
00:12:22.047 --> 00:12:36.893
Whichever contributes more to the sum is, in effect, what we would observe, it “wins.” At low temperatures, the gas history wins, and the universe is full of gas; at high temperatures, the black-hole history wins, and the universe contains a black hole.

54
00:12:37.013 --> 00:12:47.252
At one particular temperature, which physicists now call the Hawking-Page temperature, the universe switches abruptly from one to the other. This is the phase transition.

55
00:12:47.702 --> 00:13:04.595
Figure 3: Which history wins depends on temperature. Below the Hawking-Page temperature (black line), the universe is filled with a gas of particles, including gravitons; above it, the universe contains a black hole. Image description: A bar running from cold to hot.

56
00:13:04.715 --> 00:13:16.504
The cold side is blue and labeled “the graviton gas wins”; the hot side is red and labeled “the black hole wins.” A line marks the Hawking-Page temperature, where one switches to the other.

57
00:13:16.954 --> 00:13:24.420
It’s a rare case where we can see two very different shapes of spacetime competing in the sum over histories, and can say which one wins.

58
00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:45.887
This is where the holographic principle enters the story. In the 1970s, Jacob Bekenstein and Hawking found that the amount of information a black hole can hold is proportional to its surface area, not to its volume. That’s very different from ordinary matter, where every bit of volume holds roughly the same amount of information.

59
00:13:46.337 --> 00:14:09.042
In the early 1990s, Gerard ’t Hooft and Leonard Susskind proposed that this idea applies to gravity in general, that everything inside a region of space can be fully described by the information on its boundary, just as a flat hologram can display a three-dimensional image. In 1997, Juan Maldacena formalized the idea further.

60
00:14:09.162 --> 00:14:30.816
Gravity inside the anti-de Sitter bowl, he argued, is equivalent to an ordinary quantum theory of particles, with no gravity at all, existing on the rim of the bowl (the universe’s outer boundary). These are the gravity side and the quantum side of the duality we described in the introduction, they’re two languages for describing the same underlying reality.

61
00:14:31.266 --> 00:14:47.626
In the case Claude studied, the quantum theory on the rim is called N=4 super Yang-Mills theory. It’s related to the theories that describe how particles interact in the real universe, but it has extra symmetries that make it much easier for physicists to work with.

62
00:14:47.746 --> 00:15:05.595
(Claude has worked with this theory before, calculating how particles interact within it to a new level of precision.) Since the gravity side predicts two histories competing with each other, the obvious question is: does N=4 super Yang-Mills theory (the quantum side) show the same thing?

63
00:15:06.045 --> 00:15:27.190
For now, physicists can analyze this theory in detail only when its particles barely interact with each other, a condition known as weak coupling. This is the opposite end of the spectrum from where Hawking and Page’s gravitational calculation works. Weak coupling on the quantum side corresponds to the small, blurry universe on the gravity side.

64
00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:31.733
So there was no guarantee that any of Hawking and Page’s findings would hold there.

65
00:15:32.183 --> 00:15:53.909
Remarkably, they do. Bo Sundborg in 1999, and after him Ofer Aharony, Joseph Marsano, Shiraz Minwalla, Kyriakos Papadodimas, and Mark Van Raamsdonk, showed that even at weak coupling, the theory on the rim goes through a transition as it heats up. This is its counterpart of the switch from gas to black hole inside the bowl.

66
00:15:54.029 --> 00:16:00.277
But it was still unknown whether this transition is abrupt, like Hawking and Page’s, or smooth and gradual.

67
00:16:00.727 --> 00:16:19.500
When particles barely interact, physicists have to calculate how they behave one step at a time. They start with a simplified version of the theory in which particles don’t interact at all. Then they add a series of corrections, each more precise and harder to compute than the last. Physicists call these steps loops.

68
00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:39.722
In 2003, Aharony and his colleagues realized that whether the phase transition was an abrupt jump, as Hawking and Page predicted, or gradual, hinged on a single number that appeared only three loops in. Computing that number involves many thousands of terms, known as diagrams, far too many to work through by hand.

69
00:16:39.842 --> 00:16:54.155
In 2005, the same group completed the calculation for a simpler cousin of the theory and found an abrupt transition. But the version for N=4 super Yang-Mills, more than 10 times larger, was never completed.

70
00:16:54.605 --> 00:17:13.686
The scale of the computational problem, while daunting, wasn’t the only obstacle to completing the calculation. There was a conceptual issue, as well: naively adding up all the parts of the calculation gives an infinite answer, and those infinities have to be tamed carefully to extract a meaningful result.

71
00:17:14.136 --> 00:17:32.498
To solve the problem, Claude ran a small research group of agents, all copies of itself. Almost every piece of the calculation was done twice, by agents acting independently. For extra consistency checks, parts of the calculation could also be compared to previous results in the literature.

72
00:17:32.948 --> 00:17:52.546
The result Claude found is an abrupt jump as the temperature rises, exactly analogous to what Hawking and Page had found using the sum over spacetime histories. The emerging picture is very simple: the transition is abrupt at both weak and strong coupling, the blurry and the smooth ends, in the gravity description.

73
00:17:52.666 --> 00:17:56.883
The most natural possibility is that it stays abrupt everywhere in between.

74
00:17:57.333 --> 00:18:09.146
This is a beautiful outcome. It shows that a calculation using the sum over histories in gravity qualitatively matches the behavior of an ordinary quantum theory with no gravity at all.

75
00:18:09.596 --> 00:18:17.143
Many open questions remain. The abrupt transition Claude found is very much like the one we see whenever we boil water.

76
00:18:17.263 --> 00:18:34.564
One interesting feature of abrupt transitions is that the losing side of the competition can survive for a while: for instance, water can be superheated above 100°C without boiling, as you could see for yourself if you were to put a cup of extremely pure water in the microwave.

77
00:18:34.684 --> 00:18:41.724
(But please don’t do this; it’s dangerous, and Anthropic would rather not be responsible for an epidemic of scalded readers.)

78
00:18:42.174 --> 00:19:02.142
Something similar happens here. Above the Hawking-Page temperature, the hot gas of gravitons and other particles can survive for a long time in a superheated state. But at a higher temperature it can no longer survive: this is another transition, the Hagedorn transition, which is a characteristic signature of string theory.

79
00:19:02.262 --> 00:19:13.779
On the gravity side, that transition has many interesting properties of its own, and it would be valuable to check in detail whether they also appear in N=4 super Yang-Mills theory.

80
00:19:14.229 --> 00:19:36.115
Another open question concerns the other possible histories of spacetime. Hawking and Page considered three: one in which their model universe is filled with gas, one that contains a large black hole, and one containing a smaller black hole that never comes to dominate the sum over histories. In principle, there are many more possible histories.

81
00:19:36.235 --> 00:19:55.812
As far as anyone knows, none of them ever dominates. But given certain changes to the starting conditions, if the universe is set spinning, for instance, or given a fixed amount of energy or charge, then some of these histories do take over. Working out exactly which ones win, and when, is an interesting problem for the future.

82
00:19:56.612 --> 00:19:57.924
The modular bootstrap.

83
00:19:58.374 --> 00:20:19.984
As we saw earlier, one useful model universe has just two dimensions of space and one of time: it’s called “3D gravity” because it describes gravity in three spacetime dimensions. According to the principle of holographic duality, 3D gravity inside the anti-de Sitter bowl is equivalent to an ordinary quantum theory living on the bowl’s rim.

84
00:20:20.434 --> 00:20:41.213
As a model, 3D gravity is much simpler than the real thing, but it still allows for black holes and many of the same mysteries. It provides a useful laboratory for studying the sum over spacetime histories. It’s also a good place to test the idea that spacetime isn’t a basic ingredient of the universe, but emerges from something deeper.

85
00:20:41.333 --> 00:21:00.191
In general relativity, black holes look smooth. But in quantum gravity, if you zoom in to the smallest scales, the Planck length, around 10 to 35 meters, where quantum effects on gravity become impossible to ignore, black holes are made of an enormous number of tiny, individual building blocks.

86
00:21:00.311 --> 00:21:09.674
Physicists believe the same is true in our own, four-dimensional universe, but the 3D version is much easier to analyze with holographic duality.

87
00:21:10.124 --> 00:21:31.985
Claude’s results build on a method called the bootstrap, a general strategy for solving quantum theories. Counterintuitively, the strategy doesn’t try to solve a theory directly. For 3D gravity, that isn’t even possible, because nobody knows how to define the theory precisely. Instead, the bootstrap rules theories out by showing that they contradict themselves.

88
00:21:32.105 --> 00:21:37.345
Physicists scan over all possible quantum theories and try to eliminate them one by one.

89
00:21:37.795 --> 00:21:54.749
Remarkably, in many cases this method makes it possible to pinpoint a single quantum theory and calculate precisely how it behaves. The requirements of relativity and quantum mechanics are so demanding, and so delicately balanced, that together they rule out every alternative.

90
00:21:54.869 --> 00:22:13.749
In 2012, the bootstrap was used to solve a quantum field theory called the 3D critical Ising model, a simple model of magnetism that also describes water at the point where liquid and vapor become indistinguishable. Since then, it has been applied to many different quantum systems.

91
00:22:14.199 --> 00:22:37.804
In 2009, Simeon Hellerman introduced a technique called the modular bootstrap, which applies the bootstrap method to 3D gravity. Its goal is to rule out inconsistent theories of quantum gravity in three spacetime dimensions. By combining it with other bootstrap methods, physicists hope to eventually eliminate every inconsistent theory. Whatever is left must be consistent.

92
00:22:37.924 --> 00:22:54.143
There could be no consistent theories, or just one, or whole families of them. Any of these outcomes would be interesting, because it would help us understand what spacetime looks like at the Planck scale in this simplified universe, and give us hints about the Planck scale in our own.

93
00:22:54.593 --> 00:23:14.921
Here’s how the modular bootstrap works. 3D gravity in anti-de Sitter spacetime has a quantity called the central charge (c), which roughly counts how many microscopic building blocks the universe is made of. When general relativity holds, c is very large, because large black holes contain an enormous number of building blocks.

94
00:23:15.041 --> 00:23:28.223
A second quantity, M₁, is the mass of the lightest black hole. To run the bootstrap, physicists scan over every possible theory, with every possible value of c and M₁, and look for inconsistencies.

95
00:23:28.673 --> 00:23:49.520
To understand how that search works, we need to go back to the sum over spacetime histories. In 3D gravity, that means adding up different 3D shapes of spacetime. They can be simple, like a ball, or more complicated, like the donut and two-holed donut in the figure below. The spacetime is the inside of the shape, the dough inside the donut.

96
00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:56.653
Its surface, like the rim of the bowl in the earlier section, is where the quantum theory is “written,” in one fewer dimension.

97
00:23:57.103 --> 00:24:18.719
Figure 4: Three possible shapes of spacetime in 3D gravity: a ball, a donut (or torus), and a two-holed donut. The spacetime is the inside of each shape, and the quantum theory is “written” on its surface. Image description: Three shapes labeled “Spacetimes with holes”: a ball with no holes, a donut (or torus) with one hole, and a two-holed donut.

98
00:24:19.169 --> 00:24:38.217
The modular bootstrap starts with a spacetime shaped like an ordinary, one-holed donut, or torus. There are two different ways to interpret this shape, depending on which direction we call “time” and which we call “space.” In the first, the long direction around the hole is time and the other, shorter loop, around the tube, is space.

99
00:24:38.337 --> 00:24:42.777
In the second, they’re swapped: the long loop is space and the shorter loop is time.

100
00:24:43.227 --> 00:24:58.935
Figure 5: Two ways to interpret the same donut-shaped spacetime. (left) Time runs the long way around the hole, and space is represented by the shorter loop; (right) space runs the long way around, and time is represented by the shorter loop.

101
00:24:59.055 --> 00:25:14.991
Image description: Two donut shapes with an arrow between them labeled “swap space and time.” On the left, time runs the long way around the hole and space is the short loop around the tube. On the right, space runs around the hole and time is the short loop.

102
00:25:15.441 --> 00:25:32.252
In calculations like these, the length of the time loop sets the temperature: the shorter the loop, the hotter the universe. So swapping the two loops trades a cold universe for a hot one. The two interpretations correspond to the two competing histories that Hawking and Page studied.

103
00:25:32.372 --> 00:25:41.913
In 3D gravity there are no gravitons, but there is a transition similar to the one that Claude analyzed in N=4 super Yang-Mills theory.

104
00:25:42.363 --> 00:26:00.814
Now we come to the crucial step. In relativity, space and time are on an equal footing. So it can’t matter which loop we label space and which we label time. As far as relativity is concerned, the two interpretations are equivalent, and the physics must be the same for every possible experiment.

105
00:26:01.264 --> 00:26:20.086
Quantum mechanics, however, treats time differently from space. To calculate anything, you pick a direction to call time and track how the system evolves along it. So, when we calculate observable quantities in quantum gravity, the two interpretations of the donut lead to calculations that look very different.

106
00:26:20.206 --> 00:26:27.413
But in relativity, it doesn’t matter which loop is called time, so the two calculations have to give exactly the same answer.

107
00:26:27.863 --> 00:26:45.516
If we knew the theory of 3D quantum gravity, we could do both calculations and confirm that they match. We don’t, but with the bootstrap method, that isn’t a problem. Physicists scan over the possible values of c (the number of building blocks) and M₁ (the mass of the lightest black hole).

108
00:26:45.636 --> 00:27:05.757
For each pair of values, they try to prove that the theory would be inconsistent, that the two calculations on the donut couldn’t possibly give the same answer. This involves searching for a special mathematical object called a bootstrap functional. If physicists find one, then the theory is inconsistent, and those values are ruled out.

109
00:27:06.207 --> 00:27:23.992
The hope, supported by the bootstrap’s successes elsewhere, is that combining this approach with other bootstrap methods will eventually rule out most possible theories of 3D quantum gravity. The theories left standing would be the only ones consistent with both gravity and quantum mechanics.

110
00:27:24.112 --> 00:27:40.709
Physicists could then use them to explore some of the biggest questions in quantum gravity: is there only one theory? Does it rely on string theory, or are there alternatives? What are black holes made of at the microscopic level, and how do we sum over spacetime histories?

111
00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:55.935
This strategy can work in principle, but it’s very difficult in practice, because bootstrap functionals are hard to find. What matters for quantum gravity is what happens when c is large, but the larger c gets, the harder the search becomes.

112
00:27:56.055 --> 00:28:14.807
In the original paper on the modular bootstrap, Hellerman proved that in any consistent theory of 3D gravity, M₁ can be no larger than c/6, up to small corrections. Theories with a heavier lightest black hole can’t possibly be consistent with both gravity and quantum mechanics.

113
00:28:15.257 --> 00:28:37.729
The goal of the modular bootstrap is to push this ceiling as low as possible, pinning down how heavy the lightest black hole can be. Physicists write the ceiling as M₁ at most c/Λ. A lower ceiling means a larger number Λ. The results below, including Claude’s, concern the simplest and most-studied version of the method, which ignores how the black holes spin.

114
00:28:38.179 --> 00:28:57.664
Over the past 15 years, physicists have worked to lower the ceiling and pin down the theory as tightly as possible. There are two ways to do this. One is analytical, building a bootstrap functional by hand, with pen-and-paper mathematics. The other is numerical, using computers to search for functionals and optimize them.

115
00:28:57.784 --> 00:29:11.522
The results of these numerical calculations suggested that the modular bootstrap could push the ceiling down to about c/9.1. But these calculations get harder as c grows, so they couldn’t settle the question.

116
00:29:11.642 --> 00:29:34.144
On the analytical side, Tom Hartman, Dalimil Mazáč, and Leonardo Rastelli proved a ceiling of c/8.5, but because this was higher than the numerical results had suggested, this clearly wasn’t the best the modular bootstrap could do. Since then, just how tightly the modular bootstrap can constrain quantum gravity has remained an open question.

117
00:29:34.594 --> 00:29:50.207
Claude set about solving the problem by running numerical experiments and reproducing results from the literature. In doing so, it noticed a pattern that physicists had so far missed, involving the way the numerical ceiling shifted slightly as c grew.

118
00:29:50.327 --> 00:30:12.112
Based on that pattern, Claude developed a hypothesis about the behavior of the bootstrap functional, using a mathematical tool called a spectral curve. Spectral curves are well known in other areas of mathematics and physics, but had never been applied to this problem. Claude found the spectral curve that governs the modular bootstrap at large values of c.

119
00:30:12.232 --> 00:30:32.020
It then used it to prove that the lightest black hole in 3D quantum gravity can be no heavier than c/Λ, where Λ is given by an exact formula and is approximately 9.1266. It also proved that this ceiling is optimal: it’s the lowest this version of the modular bootstrap can ever go.

120
00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:37.707
Claude delivered the solution across four research notes, which it later merged into a single paper.

121
00:30:38.157 --> 00:30:57.688
This is one of the first times the bootstrap has been used to derive an exact result in the regime of quantum gravity where black holes matter. It also shows that this version of the method can’t, on its own, push the ceiling down to c/12, the mass at which black holes first appear; that will take other ingredients.

122
00:30:57.808 --> 00:31:06.064
Although it doesn’t pin down the theory of quantum gravity in three dimensions by itself, it provides a starting point and helps to narrow the search.

123
00:31:06.864 --> 00:31:07.677
Conclusion.

124
00:31:08.127 --> 00:31:29.581
These two results show Claude doing two very different kinds of research. The first is a known calculation that physicists had set aside for two decades, because the work was too long and error-prone to complete by hand. The second required a new idea: spotting a pattern others had missed and bringing in a mathematical tool from another area of physics.

125
00:31:29.701 --> 00:31:42.001
Both were carried out largely autonomously. Neither result settles the question of quantum gravity, or even comes close to doing so, but each pushes physics forward in a longstanding area of research.

